1129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 586 0 R
/T (This article examines the institutional foundations and strategic trajectories of economic integration between the Russian Federation and the Islamic Republic of Pakistan within the broader context of Eurasian cooperation. Despite a historically modest economic relationship, recent geopolitical shifts and mutual strategic interests have catalyzed a renewed impetus for bilateral engagement. This paper analyzes the evolving models of trade and investment cooperation, moving beyond ad-hoc interactions toward a more structured partnership. It investigates the core drivers of integration, including energy complementarity, infrastructure development, and shared aspirations for a multipolar world order. Concurrently, it identifies critical institutional deficits, such as the absence of a Bilateral Investment Treaty \(BIT\) and the disruptive impact of Western sanctions, which impede progress. By examining flagship projects like the Pakistan Stream gas pipeline and railway modernization initiatives, the article assesses the efficacy of existing institutional mechanisms, including the Intergovernmental Commission \(IGC\). The findings suggest that while a strong political foundation exists, translating this into substantial economic integration requires the creation of resilient financial architectures, harmonized legal frameworks, and the successful implementation of pilot projects to build trust and demonstrate viability.)
/E (This article examines the institutional foundations and strategic trajectories of economic integration between the Russian Federation and the Islamic Republic of Pakistan within the broader context of Eurasian cooperation. Despite a historically modest economic relationship, recent geopolitical shifts and mutual strategic interests have catalyzed a renewed impetus for bilateral engagement. This paper analyzes the evolving models of trade and investment cooperation, moving beyond ad-hoc interactions toward a more structured partnership. It investigates the core drivers of integration, including energy complementarity, infrastructure development, and shared aspirations for a multipolar world order. Concurrently, it identifies critical institutional deficits, such as the absence of a Bilateral Investment Treaty \(BIT\) and the disruptive impact of Western sanctions, which impede progress. By examining flagship projects like the Pakistan Stream gas pipeline and railway modernization initiatives, the article assesses the efficacy of existing institutional mechanisms, including the Intergovernmental Commission \(IGC\). The findings suggest that while a strong political foundation exists, translating this into substantial economic integration requires the creation of resilient financial architectures, harmonized legal frameworks, and the successful implementation of pilot projects to build trust and demonstrate viability.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
