/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 592 0 R
/T (The contribution of Small and Medium Enterprises \(SMEs\) in national economy is evident, as this sector consists of approximately 3.2 million enterprises in Pakistan, accounting for approximately 40% of the total GDP. Meanwhile, 6 million SMEs in Russia contribute around 22.3% to the GDP. This study elaborates on the strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, challenges, and operational framework of SME cross-border partnerships between Russia and Pakistan, including insufficient institutional support, financial constraints, limited R&D, and regional conflicts. Pakistan-Russia economic relations are expanding through frameworks such as the Shanghai Cooperation Organization and ISTC, which largely focus on trade flows, energy, and corridors. However, limited studies have been conducted on the operational role of SMEs as a key future driver of economic integration. Today, economic collaboration is export-oriented; however, the SME-to-SME sector needs attention. Additionally, this collaboration lacks policy support, government-backed institutional mechanisms, and operational frameworks, including joint chambers of commerce at the regional level and knowledge incubators. The method adapted in this study is qualitative and uses a conceptual framework centered on government-backed support and centralized coordination channels at the regional level. Additionally, a comparative analysis in the context of Pak-China collaboration is discussed as a reference case to drive the framework for Pakistan-Russia economic integration. In the future, the implementation of a policy-integrated framework and network-oriented collaborations can strengthen SME-oriented partnerships between both countries and foster an entrepreneurial culture that plays a significant role in regional economic integration.)
/E (The contribution of Small and Medium Enterprises \(SMEs\) in national economy is evident, as this sector consists of approximately 3.2 million enterprises in Pakistan, accounting for approximately 40% of the total GDP. Meanwhile, 6 million SMEs in Russia contribute around 22.3% to the GDP. This study elaborates on the strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, challenges, and operational framework of SME cross-border partnerships between Russia and Pakistan, including insufficient institutional support, financial constraints, limited R&D, and regional conflicts. Pakistan-Russia economic relations are expanding through frameworks such as the Shanghai Cooperation Organization and ISTC, which largely focus on trade flows, energy, and corridors. However, limited studies have been conducted on the operational role of SMEs as a key future driver of economic integration. Today, economic collaboration is export-oriented; however, the SME-to-SME sector needs attention. Additionally, this collaboration lacks policy support, government-backed institutional mechanisms, and operational frameworks, including joint chambers of commerce at the regional level and knowledge incubators. The method adapted in this study is qualitative and uses a conceptual framework centered on government-backed support and centralized coordination channels at the regional level. Additionally, a comparative analysis in the context of Pak-China collaboration is discussed as a reference case to drive the framework for Pakistan-Russia economic integration. In the future, the implementation of a policy-integrated framework and network-oriented collaborations can strengthen SME-oriented partnerships between both countries and foster an entrepreneurial culture that plays a significant role in regional economic integration.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [2]
>>
