1262 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 595 0 R
/T (The paper will discuss how pharmaceutical collaboration between Pakistan and Russia is being re-organized in the environment of multipolarity, financial fragmentation via sanctions, and securitization of medical supply chains, and that such pressures are likely to be contributing to the emergence of Eurasian health care corridors gradually that is integrating manufacturing, regulation, logistics, and market access in Russia, Central Asia, and South Asia. Current literature is inclined to consider pharmaceutical industrial policy, export upgrading and regional connectedness as independent realms, therefore, it fails to explain the firm-level processes by which the corridor-like terms are formed as commercial strategy is transformed by regulatory harmonization, localization incentives and geopolitical risk. This gap is addressed in the study by posing the question of how the pharmaceutical relation between Pakistan and Russia is operationalized in practice, what drives the firms to enter into partnerships and market access in the changing Eurasian regulatory frameworks, why Central Asian jurisdictions are now viewed as interim production or distribution sites and how the participants cope with compliance, transaction frictions and continuity of supplies amidst the competing geo-economic regimes. The article uses a qualitative research design \(semi-structured expert interview\) of pharmaceutical manufacturers, export managers, regulatory affairs specialists, distributors, joint-venture executives, and investment or industrial-zone stakeholders in Pakistan, Russia/EAEU-facing markets, and the selected Central Asian economies with the supplementary use of documentary research of regulatory and industrial policy frameworks. The analysis of interview and documentary data is conducted by using iterative thematic coding to detect repeat decision logics and mechanisms of building corridors. The article proposes that the formation of corridors is the result not of a bilateral initiative but rather of the coordination of incentives between states: Russian localization and procurement systems, the Pakistani diversified regulated-market strategies and Central Asian platform strategies are mechanism to give rise to new models of partnership, new practices of regulatory navigation, and new paths of logistics access, which combine in a cumulative way to create an emerging architecture of corridors in healthcare.)
/E (The paper will discuss how pharmaceutical collaboration between Pakistan and Russia is being re-organized in the environment of multipolarity, financial fragmentation via sanctions, and securitization of medical supply chains, and that such pressures are likely to be contributing to the emergence of Eurasian health care corridors gradually that is integrating manufacturing, regulation, logistics, and market access in Russia, Central Asia, and South Asia. Current literature is inclined to consider pharmaceutical industrial policy, export upgrading and regional connectedness as independent realms, therefore, it fails to explain the firm-level processes by which the corridor-like terms are formed as commercial strategy is transformed by regulatory harmonization, localization incentives and geopolitical risk. This gap is addressed in the study by posing the question of how the pharmaceutical relation between Pakistan and Russia is operationalized in practice, what drives the firms to enter into partnerships and market access in the changing Eurasian regulatory frameworks, why Central Asian jurisdictions are now viewed as interim production or distribution sites and how the participants cope with compliance, transaction frictions and continuity of supplies amidst the competing geo-economic regimes. The article uses a qualitative research design \(semi-structured expert interview\) of pharmaceutical manufacturers, export managers, regulatory affairs specialists, distributors, joint-venture executives, and investment or industrial-zone stakeholders in Pakistan, Russia/EAEU-facing markets, and the selected Central Asian economies with the supplementary use of documentary research of regulatory and industrial policy frameworks. The analysis of interview and documentary data is conducted by using iterative thematic coding to detect repeat decision logics and mechanisms of building corridors. The article proposes that the formation of corridors is the result not of a bilateral initiative but rather of the coordination of incentives between states: Russian localization and procurement systems, the Pakistani diversified regulated-market strategies and Central Asian platform strategies are mechanism to give rise to new models of partnership, new practices of regulatory navigation, and new paths of logistics access, which combine in a cumulative way to create an emerging architecture of corridors in healthcare.)
/Pg 46 0 R
