/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 641 0 R
/E (Amid intensifying great power competition and the gradual transition toward a multipolar international system Russia-Pakistan relations have undergone a noticeable reorientation. Historically characterized by limited engagement recent interactions indicate a selective convergence shaped by evolving geopolitical constraints rather than ideological alignment. This paper examines the emerging trajectory of Russia-Pakistan relations through the combined lens of neoclassical realism and strategic pragmatism. Neoclassical realism provides a framework to assess how systemic pressures such as shifting power configurations, regional instability in South Asia and Afghanistan and the erosion of US centric global influence interact with domestic political and strategic considerations to shape foreign policy choices. Strategic pragmatism further explains the issue based nature of bilateral engagement, emphasizing functional cooperation in areas including security coordination, energy diplomacy and regional connectivity without the formation of formal alliance commitments. The study argues that Russia and Pakistan are driven by overlapping but limited interests resulting in a flexible partnership that prioritizes strategic autonomy and policy adaptability. This pattern reflects a broader trend in contemporary international relations, where states pursue selective cooperation to manage uncertainty while avoiding binding obligations. By situating Russia Pakistan relations within this theoretical framework the paper contributes to a nuanced understanding of middle power diplomacy and non-alliance-based convergence in a changing global order. The findings highlight the significance of pragmatic alignment as an alternative to traditional alliance structures in an increasingly fragmented international system.)
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
