2146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 651 0 R
/E (In the early 16th century, Zahir al-Din Muhammad Babur, as a member of the Timurid clan, actively engaged in the struggle for the Timurid political legacy. However, for a number of reasons, he suffered a series of setbacks in his confrontation with his rivals. As a result, Babur left Transoxiana and, after some time, found himself in India, where he succeeded in inflicting military defeat on his opponents. Of particular significance was his victory over one of his most powerful adversaries, the Rajput military leader Rana Sangram Singh, which played a decisive role in the creation of a new Muslim state in India. It was this victory, which contributed to the expansion of the Islamic world's borders, that led to the transformation of Babur's religious imageWhile previously, authors of written sources often characterized him as an "enemy of religion and the state," over time, their assessment shifted, and he began to be perceived as a "ghazi" and "expander of the boundaries of Islam." Furthermore, Babur's decision to abolish the tamgha tax in the conquered territories and the provision of significant assistance to newly arrived Muslim settlers, as well as maintaining close ties with representatives of the Naqshbandi brotherhood and other measures contributed to strengthening his reputation as a true Muslim and patron of Islam.)
/Pg 102 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
