2226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 656 0 R
/E (Relations between Russia and Pakistan have moved from geopolitical consultations to the practical implementation of joint decisions. Following record trade turnover in 2023-2024, the ground is being paved for major investments. The main areas of development for bilateral investment ties are the fuel and energy sector, finance \(Islamic banking\), industry, and trade, including agricultural raw materials. Energy is the main driver of promising cooperation. Pakistan urgently needs energy to meet its population of 250 million. Russian companies can not only supply liquefied natural gas but also act as contractors for geological exploration and production within the country, as Pakistan lacks its own shale exploration technology. Potential resumption of the Pakistan Stream \(formerly North-South\) gas pipeline project would give Russia access to ports on the Indian Ocean. Pakistan is actively converting its financial system to Shariah standards, planning to completely abolish interest by 2028. This paves the way for attracting investment from the Middle East through the issuance of sukuk securities, particularly in infrastructure projects such as the China-Pakistan Economic Corridor. These efforts could be synchronized with the pilot project on partner financing, which began in the Russian Federation on September 1, 2023, across four republics: Tatarstan, Bashkortostan, Dagestan, and Chechnya. The pilot project aims to introduce Shariah-compliant, interest-free financial services with the participation of banks and non-credit institutions listed with the Bank of Russia. Pakistan has officially invited Russia to invest in the electric vehicle, fertilizer, and pharmaceutical sectors. Discussions are underway for the full modernization of Pakistan Steel Mills, which involves technology transfer and the creation of modern facilities.Currently, there are challenges that hinder the successful development of investment cooperation between the countries. Payments under sanctions are the main obstacle. Even after an agreement is signed, Pakistani banks often refuse to process payments for fear of secondary US sanctions. The technological gap and energy market volatility also pose serious challenges.)
/Pg 107 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
