2277 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 659 0 R
/E (Unlike traditional military doctrines of states, which are collectively developed within defense ministries, in Pakistan, each change of Chief of Army Staff is often accompanied by a rethinking of strategic course, expressed in an informally codified "doctrine" designated by the name of the current Chief of Staff \(for example, Q. Bajwa or A. Munir\). This term has appeared in expert discourse relatively recently and has not received sufficient theoretical development. However, it appears that studying this phenomenon offers significant theoretical and practical value for Pakistani studies. Moreover, studying this aspect is particularly relevant in light of the development of bilateral relations between Russia and Pakistan: in recent years, positive dynamics have been observed in military-technical cooperation, requiring an in-depth understanding of processes within the Pakistani Armed Forces to build security relations. This paper analyzes the development of such doctrines, their constituent elements, as well as the main determining factors and their impact on Russian-Pakistani relations. To this end, we propose using a comparative institutional approach, as well as a qualitative content analysis of statements by individual chiefs of staff over the years. The author believes that, firstly, this phenomenon demonstrates how, in a context of weak civilian institutions, the influence of the personal views of a member of the armed forces \(more importantly, the army, as the most influential branch of the armed forces\) on the country's domestic and foreign policy is formed. Secondly, through the conceptual framework of the chiefs of staff's doctrines, we can examine the process of institutionalizing foreign policy in the discourse of the generals. Finally, we can trace how the personalization of strategic thinking can be a sustainable mechanism for maintaining political control and adapting to regional challenges, such as the escalating conflict in Afghanistan, opening up new niches for Russia in the sphere of military-technical cooperation. As a result of this analysis, the author concludes that in recent years, the change of Pakistan's Chief of Army Staff has significantly influenced the search for a balance between interactions with international actors such as China, Russia, and the United States.)
/Pg 110 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
