/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 660 0 R
/E (Cultural diplomacy forms a vital, yet historically underutilized, foundation for building a sustainable Russia-Pakistan partnership in a globalized world that values unity in diversity. It begins by framing contemporary international relations around the imperative of intercultural dialogue, as endorsed by UN and UNESCO, to achieve global harmony. Russia and Pakistan have a deep rooted historical linkage as the Mongols, Lodhis and Mughals who ruled the sub-continent for centuries belonged to CARs that was a part of USSR. Even during Cold War there was the formalization of a strong cultural and scholarly exchange with a 1965 agreement which in turn saw the formation of bilateral friendship societies, massive literary translations of the works of M. Iqbal, F. A. Faiz, S. H. Manto, etc. Establishment of Russian language units in Pakistan enhanced people-to-people connections accompanied by film festivals, artistic tours and a Twin City relationship between Dushanbe and Lahore, as well as the Soviet-sponsored higher education of hundreds of Pakistani students. This thaw yields a rich, positive legacy driving scholars and diplomats towards rebuilding bridges through intellectual collaboration, founded on the historical footings in this polycentric world. Pakistan is looking for partners and looking in the neighborhood Russia provides a great opportunity in the East due to proximity and historical linkage. This study utilizes a qualitative approach relying on research articles, press release, statements of government officials and MoUs between the two states as primary resource, contending that reactivating and expanding upon this historical people-to-people template through academic centers, revitalized cultural societies and leveraging new social phenomena like intercultural interactions is essential. Such diplomatic and social rapprochement is expected to transform the bilateral relations from strategic pragmatism into a genuine, resilient friendship, thereby integrating both nations into a cooperative global social space.)
/Pg 111 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
