/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 661 0 R
/E (In today's global era of swift power shift, Russia and Pakistan are finding common interests via diplomatic dialogues, cultural exchanges and shared values. Fields like humanitarian aid, education, arts, language programs, youth initiatives, academic swaps, and people-to-people ties offer cultural diversity. Ideas of academic collaboration, joint research projects, scholarships, or joint degree programs facilitate sharing of knowledge, culture and experience.Gender equality is constitutionally protected in both states acknowledging women as an important participant in society and family life. However, there are strict barriers like sex or gender-based workplace harassment worsened by legal, institutional, and social problems with added patriarchal resistance. This paper addresses a key question: How can Anti-Harassment Laws and Enforcement mechanisms in Russia and Pakistan contribute to develop inclusivity for women in academic institutions ensuring Constitutional mandates of gender equality? This paper is doctrinal legal research focused to critically analyse the Russian Pakistani laws, reports, and insights from stakeholders highlighting socio-legal hurdles in effective implementation at academic institutions. The objective is to recommend practical reforms for anti-harassment policies in workplaces, with a focus on fostering safe, harassment-free academic cultures empowering women. Major recommendations include First, Revising the legal landscape of Workplace harassment Laws- suggesting model law for Russia. Second, set up one unanimous anti-harassment policy mandatory for all universities. Third, introduction of dedicated "Safe Campus Committees" with anonymous reporting apps in multiple languages. Fourth, mandatory training on workplace harassment and cultural sensitivity and surveys as pilot programs under SCO or BRICS youth forums for awareness ending under reporting.The way forward will eventually lead to strengthen trust, promise women's involvement, and foster sustainable development across Eurasia, harmonising with broader goals of cultural collaboration for regional stability. This will also be a legal and social attempt to empower women across borders.)
/Pg 112 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
