/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 668 0 R
/E (The emergence of a multipolar global economic model in the 21st century is accompanied by the strengthening of Eurasia's role as an independent macroeconomic center. Under these circumstances, cooperation between the Russian Federation and the Islamic Republic of Pakistan is acquiring strategic significance in the context of transforming trade flows, energy routes, and financial mechanisms. Both countries are integrated into key regional structures, including the Shanghai Cooperation Organization, the Eurasian Economic Union, BRICS, and the Organization of Islamic Cooperation, which provides an institutional foundation for deepening interaction. Bilateral trade turnover has demonstrated steady positive momentum: in 2023, trade volume exceeded $1 billion for the first time, with Russian exports amounting to approximately $782 million and imports from Pakistan reaching approximately $289 million, reflecting significant growth potential in economic ties between the two countries. According to the Russian Ministry of Economic Development, over the past five years, Russian exports to Pakistan have increased by 35%, while overall trade between the two countries has increased by 15% over the same period, reinforcing the trend of deepening economic cooperation. The trade structure is complementary: Russia supplies grain, energy, and industrial goods, while Pakistan supplies light industrial products, agricultural goods, and textiles. This factor reduces the direct competitiveness of the relationship and creates conditions for sustainable trade growth. Energy cooperation is becoming a key element of collaboration, as the parties engage in dialogue on expanding energy supplies and participating in the modernization of energy infrastructure. At the same time, transport and logistics cooperation is intensifying, aimed at developing southern Eurasian routes, opening up additional opportunities for access to Central Asian and Indian Ocean markets. The financial component of cooperation is particularly important in the context of global instability: the transition to settlements in national currencies and the development of alternative payment mechanisms could enhance the resilience of bilateral trade. The financial component of interaction is becoming especially important in the context of global instability: the transition to settlements in national currencies and the development of alternative payment mechanisms can increase the stability of bilateral trade. The scenario analysis for development up to 2036 includes an optimistic scenario with deepening institutional integration and increased trade turnover, a baseline scenario of moderate expansion, and a risk scenario associated with a slowdown due to geopolitical and economic imbalances. Given current trends, the baseline scenario, in which cooperation between Russia and Pakistan gradually strengthens, playing a significant role in the Eurasian economic architecture, appears most likely.)
/Pg 119 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
