2716 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 685 0 R
/E (The present international order is fragmenting at a rapid pace, as seen by rising US China rivalry, the geopolitical consequences from the Ukraine conflict, sanctions driven economic blocs, and the rise of regional power politics. Secondary states in this evolving framework are no longer passive beneficiaries of systemic pressures; instead, they have measurable strategic agency. This study contends that Pakistan is a key example of adaptable statecraft in an evolving multipolar system.)
/Pg 136 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2717 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 685 0 R
/E ( The paper uses a hybrid analytical framework that combines neorealist balancing theory, hedging literature, and strategic autonomy paradigms to assess Pakistan's foreign policy diversification between 2014 and 2025. It quantitatively maps changes in diplomatic engagement, defense collaboration, trade realignment, and energy discussions, particularly in light of the changing relationship with Russia. The study identifies three measurable indicators of agency \(1\) diversifying security partnerships, \(2\) expanding energy diplomacy beyond conventional blocs, and \(3\) calibrated neutrality in great-power crises.)
/Pg 136 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
