2754 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 687 0 R
/E (Russia and Pakistan have had scarce connections in the area of media and communication studies. However, the experience of participating in academic conferences and the IAMCR Spring School on media and communication studies \(2022-2025\) allows for firmly saying that the road of collaboration should be taken. There are political, technological, professional, and academic similarities, including similar issues to tackle, that would foster such collaboration. Thus, both countries are emerging as poles in the multi-polar world. They have developed media systems and mediatized societies, but journalism in both countries experiences large-scale impact of the state and struggles for survival. Moreover, media studies in both countries rely on Western concepts which rarely fit to local contexts, and PhD students equally suffer from under-training in terms of getting through to major world journals. We explore the obstacles for the current development of collaboration in media industry and academe, as well as suggest three-level measures to develop mid-range projects, with the goal of developing the field itself, trust, and culture of collaboration. We argue that national associations of media scholars, MCAP and NAMMI, both very active nationally, should play the leading role in organizing co-operation between media scholars. We also urge the embassies and think tanks to take on more active roles in supporting the existing initiatives to make them long-term points of collaboration growth.)
/Pg 138 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
