/Title (This article argues how the province of Balochistan might influence and possibly strengthen Russia-Pakistan relations in the context of the changing global order, being multipolar, geoeconomically competitive and rebalancing regional alliances. This paper frames Balochistan as a sub-state strategic space whose geographical position, sea-access, and the infrastructure of corridors, makes it a possible nexus between the Eurasian orientation of Russia and the connectivity goals of Pakistan. The study uses a geoeconomic statecraft model combined with Regional Security Complex Theory \(RSCT\) to discuss the ways in which infrastructure development, energy transit corridors and port expansion in Balochistan are connected to regional security interdependence patterns. It analyzes the future of Russian involvement in energy pipelines, modernization of ports and linkage of North and South transportation between Russia and Central Asia to the Arabian Sea via Pakistan. At the same time, it evaluates the nature of securitization, governance limitations and insurgency, vulnerabilities in Balochistan that predetermines the viability and sustainability of such collaboration. The paper claims that Balochistan is a strategically important, but structurally weak point in the broader Eurasian security system. Although geopolitical factors, such as the long-term strategic alliance between Russia and India, sanctions regimes and the general rivalry between the great powers, do not provide an avenue to the formal alliance, the project based geoeconomic cooperation in Balochistan provides a viable route to gradual rapprochement. This paper concludes that the potential of the province to enable long-lasting Russia-Pakistan relations is conditional on how internal stabilization interacts with calculated desecuritization as well as alignment of strategies of Eurasia connectivity in a progressively elastic multipolar order.)
/Dest [42 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 20585 0 R
>>
endobj
