/Title (Pakistan has adapted its foreign policy in accordance with new geopolitical dynamics and a desire to reduce its dependency on the US. This has motivated Pakistan to seek economic partnerships in the Eurasian region, particularly through efforts to strengthen ties with Russia via bilateral relations and regional frameworks such as the Eurasian Economic Union \(EAEU\) and the Shanghai Cooperation Organization \(SCO\). This study aims to investigate the drivers of the Russia-Pakistan rapprochement. It addresses two fundamental questions: first, which theories of international relations explain the course of Russia-Pakistan relations? Second, what are the potential outcomes for the region from the strengthening of Russia-Pakistan ties? This study employs a qualitative approach to assess the motives behind the Russia-Pakistan reorientation through the lens of enduring theories of international relations. It uses realism to explore fundamental factors such as Pakistan's diversification away from the US and Russia's efforts to neutralize Western sanctions. It applies liberalism to examine Pakistan's gradual foreign policy shift and its increased engagement in regional economic and political partnerships such as the EAEU and the SCO. Furthermore, it draws on constructivism to understand the mutual narratives of both countries on multipolarity and the strengthening of Global South camaraderie. This research is based on primary sources including official documents from the governments of Russia and Pakistan, reports released by the Eurasian Economic Commission, statements issued by the Shanghai Cooperation Organization, and bilateral trade data between Russia and Pakistan. This study also take leverage from secondary sources such as academic articles and reports by Russian and Pakistani scholars and their respective institutions. Prefatory findings reveal that all three theories elaborate different motives for the rapprochement of Russia and Pakistan. Realism stresses geopolitical pressures, liberalism draws on institutional engagement, while constructivism highlights the role of the ideational world. The significance of this study is that it helps us understand the complex nature of Russia-Pakistan realignment, which has been accelerated by multiple realities of the contemporary period.)
/Dest [48 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 20638 0 R
>>
endobj
