20670 0 obj
<<
/Title (This paper explores how Russia and Pakistan use the institutional framework of the Shanghai Cooperation Organization \(SCO\) to advance common counterterrorism objectives and promote regional stability in Eurasia. Situated at the crossroads of Central and South Asia, the two states face overlapping security challenges, including international terrorism, extremism, drug trafficking, and the spillover effects of instability in Afghanistan. The central research question guiding this study is: To what extent has SCO membership enabled meaningful Russia-Pakistan security coordination, and what are the limits of this cooperation.? The paper adopts a qualitative, case study approach. It examines key SCO documents, joint declarations, and summit messages, with a particular focus on the activities of the Regional Anti-Terrorism Structure \(RATS\). It also analyzes joint military exercises, intelligence-sharing practices, and capacity-building initiatives involving Moscow and Islamabad under the Shanghai Cooperation Organization. The paper argues that the SCO has provided Moscow and Islamabad with a useful multilateral platform for institutionalizing dialogue, building confidence, and conducting joint counterterrorism engagements that would be politically more sensitive in a purely bilateral format. At the same time, their coordination is limited by broader geopolitical rivalries, divergent security priorities, and a long-standing deficit in mutual trust. The analysis shows that, although SCO-based mechanisms have not transformed the security landscape, they have contributed significantly to regional stability by fostering habits of cooperation and creating channels for crisis communication. The paper concludes with an outline of policy recommendations for strengthening Russia-Pakistan cooperation within the Shanghai Cooperation Organization, including closer engagement with RATS, more frequent and specialized joint exercises, and better coordination on security measures related to Afghanistan.)
/Dest [51 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 20668 0 R
>>
endobj
